WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:02.580
I’ve never owned a precon.

00:00:02.580 --> 00:00:05.860
Wizards of the Coast has been releasing preconstructed
commander decks every year since I started

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.910
playing in 2011, and with increasing frequency,
and I’ve just kept on ignoring them.

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:15.260
The reason for this is simple enough—I didn’t
start playing commander until 2014, at which

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.500
point I felt like a skilled enough player
to build my own mono black removal pile to

00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:19.900
piss off my friends.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:24.800
However, as I’ve continued to make videos
and exist more in the MTG-space, it’s become

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:29.420
increasingly clear to me that precons play
a major role in the way people learn and conceptualize

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:33.590
the EDH format, so I figure I should take
a moment to engage with the world of precons.

00:00:33.590 --> 00:00:37.750
I polled my patrons, and they thought I should
take a look at the Blame Game precon, so I’m

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.250
going to do just that—I’m going to breakdown
what I think the deck is trying to do, how

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.250
well it’s accomplishing that plan, and some
potential improvement routes for making it

00:00:46.250 --> 00:00:47.460
function better.

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:50.489
Let’s first break down what the commander
is offering.

00:00:50.489 --> 00:00:54.800
Nelly’s first ability contains the “suspect”
component, which offers immediate evasion

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.050
by disabling a blocker, as well as the goad
component, which offers a way to keep threats

00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:03.410
away from our life total as long as Nelly
keeps attacking, and guarantees that our opponents

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.210
will be attacking each other.

00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:09.280
The second ability is a source of draw that
can offer up to three cards per turn cycle,

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:13.060
while also feeding opponents that resource
as a reward for hitting each other, whether

00:01:13.060 --> 00:01:15.610
due to being compelled or otherwise.

00:01:15.610 --> 00:01:19.850
This is a pretty fun package—the first ability
will scale to great effect over time, and

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:23.940
the second ability offers some fun politics
gameplay and discourages our opponents from

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:28.200
removing Nelly for fear of losing out on those
sweet, sweet cards.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.229
Less to the point of this video, the flavor
here is great—a commander that is constantly

00:01:32.229 --> 00:01:35.950
accusing people of murder, which is causing
the accused to lash out in order to curry

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.619
favor as long as Nelly is around, good stuff.

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:43.009
To go along with the commander, the deck is
running lots of cards with the intent of getting

00:01:43.009 --> 00:01:46.430
our opponents to attack each other rather
than attacking us.

00:01:46.430 --> 00:01:49.350
There are a bunch of other goad cards, there
are cards that give people advantages for

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:54.230
attacking our opponents, there are cards that
punish people for attacking us, and so on.

00:01:54.230 --> 00:01:57.799
Basically, this deck is trying to control
the midgame by getting our opponents to fight

00:01:57.799 --> 00:02:02.409
amongst themselves, and Nelly draws us additional
cards when they do this.

00:02:02.409 --> 00:02:06.380
This card draw should hopefully fuel a powerful
late game, where a suite of bigger creatures

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:10.429
means that once our opponents are dropping
in life total and dwindling in number, we

00:02:10.429 --> 00:02:13.430
can take control of the game with our boardstate.

00:02:13.430 --> 00:02:17.340
So that’s the gameplan, but how effectively
can the deck accomplish it?

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:20.430
Well, there are a handful of things that the
deck is doing decently well when it comes

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:21.920
to fundamentals.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.840
It has eight cards that I can broadly label
as “draw”, which isn’t a bad number

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:31.099
when paired with a commander that will likely
be drawing 1-2 cards per turn cycle.

00:02:31.099 --> 00:02:35.069
There are also eleven cards that one could
label as ramp, which is also a solid number,

00:02:35.069 --> 00:02:38.739
and this number also works well with a creature
base that includes a number of higher-costed

00:02:38.739 --> 00:02:40.019
beaters.

00:02:40.019 --> 00:02:44.370
Finishing off the final opponent is something
I’d naturally worry about for a goad deck,

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:48.260
but I think the creature base is just beefy
enough that the deck should be able to finish

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:52.440
off a game a lot of the time, as long as the
commander has been getting in the desired

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:54.280
number of draw triggers.

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:58.440
The deck contains nine cards that I’d call
removal, and although I’m a little concerned

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:03.190
at how high-cost most of it is, I’m mostly
willing to overlook that due to how many other

00:03:03.190 --> 00:03:06.909
options the deck has for interacting with
opposing creatures.

00:03:06.909 --> 00:03:11.370
The deck includes ten extra goad cards, which
bolster the commander’s card draw and will

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:17.370
also help keep the heat off us even if our
commander-based goad engine goes offline.

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.819
What I don’t like is the amount of other
player-protective cards in this deck.

00:03:21.819 --> 00:03:25.819
I tallied up all the cards that discourage
or disallow attacking us in some way, and

00:03:25.819 --> 00:03:28.580
the total for these was 33 cards.

00:03:28.580 --> 00:03:31.960
One third of the deck is trying to get our
opponents to attack each other instead of

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:33.069
us.

00:03:33.069 --> 00:03:37.629
And I get that that’s a big thing the deck
is trying to do, but 33 deck slots is way

00:03:37.629 --> 00:03:42.580
too many for a task that won’t win or prevent
our opponents from winning on its own.

00:03:42.580 --> 00:03:47.019
Like okay, the ideal scenario is that you’ll
have key opposing creatures being tossed around

00:03:47.019 --> 00:03:52.890
wildly with opposing gameplans and life totals
crumbling before our very eyes, but how often

00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:54.960
will this actually happen?

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:58.709
There will be games where our opponents are
developing horrifying wincons that don’t

00:03:58.709 --> 00:04:03.680
use combat, and we just sit there twiddling
our thumbs as we goad their utility 2/3s into

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:07.900
Johnny-no-board over there and draw them into
the gas they need to murder us.

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:11.480
My guess is that different defensive cards
are meant to serve somewhat different purposes

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.730
throughout the game, but this is quite inefficient
slots-wise.

00:04:14.730 --> 00:04:21.359
Additionally, and this is kind of a precon
classic, but the manabase is bad.

00:04:21.359 --> 00:04:27.810
37 lands might be an okay count in the abstract,
but it includes 11 solely-colorless-producing

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:29.449
utility lands.

00:04:29.449 --> 00:04:34.650
This results in the deck having 20 white sources
and 17 red sources between its artifacts and

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:35.650
lands.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:40.700
That’s right, this 37 land and 6 rock two
color mana base has symbol numbers that are

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:46.720
similar to those of the budget 30 land, four
color mana base from my lands video.

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:51.650
And this deck has like 10 double red cards
in it—you need 26 red sources to consistently

00:04:51.650 --> 00:04:56.419
cast a Vengeful Ancestor on curve, and here
we are with 17.

00:04:56.419 --> 00:04:58.070
Wizards please.

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:00.880
Just put things that tap for colored mana
in your precons.

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:02.530
Just do it, I know you can.

00:05:02.530 --> 00:05:03.990
Thank you.

00:05:03.990 --> 00:05:09.930
I ran 15 playtests with the deck, and my previous
hunches were pretty well backed up.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.840
The deck can have a decent mid game curve-out
with solid ramping and drawing capabilities,

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:18.230
but it frequently has mana issues and also
frequently gets stuck with a hand full of

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.620
nothing but niche defensive cards.

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:24.600
Another issue that was on my radar but which
became much more clear in playtesting was

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:29.510
that the deck has only a handful of good three
drops, and basically no two drops other than

00:05:29.510 --> 00:05:31.180
mana rocks.

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.660
When I playtested it I was aggressively mulliganing
for early plays, which led to the deck having

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:40.550
what I’d consider to be a decent curve out
8 of the 15 playtests, but I imagine a new

00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:44.771
player piloting the deck would probably happily
keep a more average hand for the deck, one

00:05:44.771 --> 00:05:49.889
that plays nothing on turn 1 and 2, a weird
enchantment on turn 3, and commander turn

00:05:49.889 --> 00:05:50.889
4.

00:05:50.889 --> 00:05:55.080
Which, I guess might work out in a game with
other precons, but leaving your two drop slot

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.710
on the table in a two color deck is not good
and will hurt your chances of winning.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:04.060
So yeah, there are definitely issues here,
but overall, this deck has a very fun, useful

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:08.720
commander and a pretty solid midgame plan
of influencing the attacks of the game and

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:10.750
leveraging politics to its favor.

00:06:10.750 --> 00:06:14.840
It’s pretty well-rounded in most areas,
with the three standout issues being manabase,

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:19.139
mana curve, and an excess of player-protective
cards.

00:06:19.139 --> 00:06:21.270
So how do we go about tuning it up?

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:25.370
For improving this deck, I want to maintain
the affordability and spirit of the deck,

00:06:25.370 --> 00:06:28.030
but smooth out some of the rough edges.

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:31.610
First of all, I would cut a bunch of defensive
cards, because they are taking up too much

00:06:31.610 --> 00:06:33.330
goddamn space in this deck.

00:06:33.330 --> 00:06:37.680
Specifically, I dislike the ones whose use
cases are too narrow, whose effect is too

00:06:37.680 --> 00:06:41.990
limited, or which cost too much mana, and
there are about a quarter of these that I’d

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:44.120
cut right off the bat.

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:47.760
From there I think it’s up to the player
to some extent—I opted to cut an additional

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.280
six for my final decklist.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:53.789
In their place there are a number of things
that could be added, and I think a good option

00:06:53.789 --> 00:06:57.550
to somewhat fill the same role is cards that
grant haste.

00:06:57.550 --> 00:07:02.870
If Nelly dies on declare combat step, well
that sucks for us, but if she dies any other

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:07.010
time we want to be able to play her again
and keep her wall of goad up.

00:07:07.010 --> 00:07:11.520
I’d also add a bit of board protection in—if
Nelly gets destroyed we’re vulnerable to

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:15.840
attacks, and if the board gets wiped we lose
basically all momentum.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:18.129
Next up, let’s talk manabase.

00:07:18.129 --> 00:07:23.039
The goal here is simple—we want several
more symbols of each color, and to get there

00:07:23.039 --> 00:07:26.620
we cut colorless lands and rocks for ones
that produce colored mana.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:32.680
I like a lot of the utility lands in principle—evasion
is good, graveyard hate is good, draw is good—so

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:37.550
I’m going to start with the ones I see as
adding basically nothing to the deck.

00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:42.590
Temple of the False God is weird and very
bad if you’re running less than, like, 43

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:44.190
other lands.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:48.300
Reliquary Tower and Thought Vessel are next
up on my shit list—this deck doesn’t draw

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:51.960
insane numbers of cards, and it also isn’t
doesn’t need to hold up a bunch of interaction

00:07:51.960 --> 00:07:53.300
and niche tech pieces.

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:58.560
I’d also cut Kher Keep, and I’d consider
cutting some number of Scavenger Grounds,

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:03.639
Throne of the High City, and Sunhome if the
deck was going to stay at the same land count,

00:08:03.639 --> 00:08:06.979
though these cards at least have some identifiable
purpose here.

00:08:06.979 --> 00:08:11.069
Speaking of land count, I think there are
two options for this deck with regard to mana

00:08:11.069 --> 00:08:12.080
curve.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.129
Option A would be to keep the higher curve,
add a couple lands, and add 4-5 two cost mana

00:08:17.129 --> 00:08:22.440
rocks to make sure we’re able to swing with
the commander on turn 4 as often as possible.

00:08:22.440 --> 00:08:25.879
I’d also fill out the deck with a few more
beaters in order to better guarantee that

00:08:25.879 --> 00:08:29.560
we’ll have the raw power to finish off the
final player.

00:08:29.560 --> 00:08:33.860
Option B would be to drop the curve, and add
a bunch of 2 and 3 drop creatures that could

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:36.709
lead us to having a more aggressive gameplan.

00:08:36.709 --> 00:08:40.279
I think either of these two gameplans would
be valid, but in my list I went for option

00:08:40.279 --> 00:08:44.691
A, as it seemed more in-line with the original
plan of the deck.

00:08:44.691 --> 00:08:48.130
Though you could certainly make a much better
deck with this commander, my goal here was

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:52.820
to only swap out 20 cards, to scrape out a
bit of the precon jank factor and make the

00:08:52.820 --> 00:08:54.130
deck play more smoothly.

00:08:54.130 --> 00:08:58.750
I posted both the original deck as well as
the improved version I made in the description,

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:01.980
so you can check out the changes I made and
let me know in the comments if you think I

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:04.790
utterly bastardized the entire point of the
deck.

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:08.280
This is also a bit different than most of
my other videos, so you can also let me know

00:09:08.280 --> 00:09:11.680
if this sort of content is something you want
to see more of in the future.

00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:12.779
Alright, bye now.

